.TH OTPME-SHELL 1 "2026-10-02" "OTPme 0.3.0a279" "OTPme Manual"
.SH NAME
otpme-shell \- get a shell on a host
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B otpme-shell
[\fIoptions\fR]
.I host
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B otpme-shell
gives you a shell on a host of your realm.
You do not need to reach the host over the network, and the host does not need an open SSH port.
The host connects out to a node of the realm, and your shell goes through that node.
.PP
This is for a host you cannot reach otherwise.
A machine behind NAT, a machine in a customer network, a machine whose sshd is broken.
.PP
What happens when you run it:
.IP 1. 4
A \fBremote_access\fR job is created for the host and signed with your sign key.
It is a configuration job like any other, see
.BR otpme\-deploy (1).
.IP 2. 4
The host hears about the job and connects out to a node.
.IP 3. 4
You connect out to the same node.
Both sides meet there under the UUID of the job.
.IP 4. 4
The host and your terminal speak SSH to each other through that node.
.PP
The node only moves bytes.
It cannot read the session.
.SS What you need
You must be logged in:
.PP
.RS
.B otpme\-tool login \fIuser\fR
.RE
.PP
The job is signed with your sign key, so a shell is always asked for by somebody.
.PP
You need an SSH agent with a key in it.
The agent is the same one you use for
.BR ssh (1).
.PP
The host must trust you for this job type.
Add the signer on the host once:
.PP
.RS
.B otpme\-tool add_signer \-\-signer\-type remote_access 'user|example.org/berlin/alice'
.RE
.PP
You must also be allowed to create jobs for the host.
This is set with the config parameter \fBallow_confd_jobs\fR.
.PP
A \fBremote_access\fR signer can get a root shell on the host.
Only add signers you trust with root access.
.SS Which key gets you in
Your SSH agent offers its keys and the host takes the first one it allows.
The host allows the keys of its own \fBauthorized_keys\fR file for the user the shell runs as.
Those are the same keys
.B otpme\-get\-authorized\-keys
hands to sshd.
.PP
So nothing has to be set up for a session.
The key you log into the host with is the key you get a shell with.
A key taken away from a user is taken away here with it.
.PP
With
.B \-\-user
the keys of that user are used, not root's.
.PP
A key is not used if its \fBauthorized_keys\fR line limits what the key may do.
.B command=
means the key is not for a shell.
.B from=
means not from here, and a session through a node does not come from where you are.
.BR no\-pty ,
.B restrict
and the others each say something this does not do.
Such a key is skipped and the job log says so.
.SS A key the job brings along
With
.B \-\-throwaway\-key
a new key pair is made for this one session.
The job carries its public half, and then the host allows that key and no other.
The key exists for the minutes the job lives and is written nowhere.
.PP
This way in is weaker, and not because the key is new.
It is weaker because it needs one thing where the ordinary way needs two.
.PP
The ordinary way needs the signature of the job \fBand\fR a key in the \fBauthorized_keys\fR of the host.
Take either away and the door is shut.
A job that brings its own key needs only the signature: it names the key it will be entered with, so whoever can sign such a job for the host has a shell.
.PP
So the host says who may ask for it, and it says so per signer.
On the host, as root:
.PP
.RS
.B otpme\-tool add_signer \-\-signer\-type remote_access \-\-allow\-throwaway\-key 'user|example.org/berlin/alice'
.RE
.PP
Or with a job, which needs a \fBsigner_mgmt\fR signer, and such a signer can do anything to the host anyway:
.PP
.RS
.B otpme\-deploy add \-\-host client1 \-\-arg type=remote_access \-\-arg 'signer=user|example.org/berlin/alice' \-\-arg allow_throwaway_key=yes signer_mgmt add_signer
.RE
.PP
Without it the host refuses the job and says so.
That is a refusal and not a failure: the job stays, and it runs as soon as the signer has the permission.
.PP
.B otpme\-tool show_signer
has a column for it, so you can see which signers may.
Use \fB\-\-throwaway\-key\fR when you have no agent, or when you want a shell that no stored key can open.
.SS How the host is checked
A node brings two sides together.
Nothing stops a node from bringing the wrong ones together.
.PP
So the host signs a line that says which SSH host key it is about to use.
It signs with the key it joined the realm with.
.B otpme-shell
checks that line against what the tree says about the host you asked for.
This happens before a word of SSH is spoken.
.PP
If it does not match, nothing is sent and you get an error.
.PP
That alone says which key the session is with, not that the key belongs to the host that named it.
A host may name any key, including one of a third host, and then pass the session through to that host: everything above still holds, and you are working on a machine you never asked for.
.PP
So once the session stands, the host signs the identity of that session as well, again with the key it joined the realm with.
Only the two ends of a session can work that identity out \(em it is the hash of their key exchange, and the shared secret of that exchange is part of it.
A host that only passes the session on never learns it and has nothing to send.
.PP
Both checks have to pass before the shell starts.
.PP
This is what
.B known_hosts
does for
.BR ssh (1),
with the realm in place of a file: there is no first connection you have to trust, and no warning when a host is reinstalled.
.PP
Both checks rest on the key the host was joined with, read from this host's copy of the tree \(em so whoever can change the tree can change which key a session is checked against.
Set \fBPIN_HOST_KEYS\fR (see
.BR otpme.conf (5))
and they cannot change it here: the key of a host is written down on the first sync that brings it, and one that differs does not replace it.
A session to that host does not open until you take the new key over with
.B otpme\-tool update_host_keys
as root, which shows you what changed.
.PP
On a host, a session to another host needs \fBPIN_HOST_KEYS\fR.
Without it a host gets only its own host object, so it has no key for any other host.
A session to a node works either way.
.SS How long it lasts
.B \-\-expiry
says how long the request stays good.
Default is 5 minutes, the host allows at most 1 hour.
It is the time in which the host has to pick the job up.
It is not the length of the session.
.PP
A session lasts as long as you work in it.
There is no limit, and no limit on how much data goes through it.
.PP
Both sides ask each other every 30 seconds whether the other is still there.
A session whose far end is gone ends in minutes instead of hanging.
.SS More than one at a time
A host runs one configuration job at a time.
A shell is the exception: it runs beside other jobs, and other jobs run beside it.
A host carries up to 8 sessions at the same time.
.PP
So two people can have a shell on the same host, and a package can be installed while somebody is logged in.
.SS Which node you meet at
Both sides meet at the master node of the site of the host.
They reach it over the management address of that site, which is the floating IP of its master.
.PP
The daemon that brings them together is \fBracd\fR, on port 2031.
It runs on the master node and nowhere else: it is started when a node takes the master role and stopped when it loses it.
Both sides need to reach that address.
.PP
There is nothing to choose here, so there is no option for it.
.SS A host in another site
Name the site with the global option \fB\-s\fR:
.PP
.RS
.B otpme\-shell \-s \fIsite host\fR
.RE
.PP
That one option moves the whole command to that site.
The job is made by the management daemon there, because that is where the connection goes, and only the nodes of a site hand a job to a host of that site.
The host is looked up there as well, and both sides meet at the master of that site.
.PP
Nothing else changes.
The job is signed with your key here, and the host holds the signature against the signers it has \(em so the host needs a signer for you (\fBotpme\-tool add_signer\fR), the same as a host of your own site.
The two sides show each other a certificate, and those are good across the realm: a host trusts the CA of the realm and the CA of every site in it.
.PP
Without \fB\-s\fR the command works against your own site, so a host of another site is not found there.
.PP
An open session holds the master failover off while it lasts.
So a shell that is left open overnight keeps the master role where it is.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-user \fIuser\fR
Run the shell as this user.
Default is \fBroot\fR.
The user must exist on the host.
.br
Lower case, up to 32 characters, and it may hold digits, \fB_\fR, \fB\-\fR and \fB.\fR, for example \fBfirstname.lastname\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-command \fIcommand\fR
Run this instead of a shell.
The host runs this and nothing else, whatever the client asks for.
.TP
.B \-\-expiry \fItime\fR
How long the request stays good, for example \fB30s\fR, \fB5m\fR or \fB1h\fR.
Default \fB5m\fR, at most \fB1h\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-throwaway\-key
Make a new key for this session instead of using the SSH agent.
.br
The host allows this only for a signer that was given the permission by hand.
See \fBA key the job brings along\fR.
.SS Giving up on it
Press Ctrl+C while you wait for the host.
It works from the moment you asked for the shell until the session is up.
.PP
Once the session is up, Ctrl+C belongs to the shell at the other end, the same as in
.BR ssh (1).
.PP
The request is not taken back by giving up.
It stands until it expires, and asking again uses it, so there is nothing to clean up.
Take it back with
.B otpme\-deploy cancel
and the UUID it printed, if you want it gone now.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B otpme-shell
exits with the exit status of the shell or of the command.
.PP
It exits with \fB255\fR when the connection failed and not the command.
This is what
.BR ssh (1)
does, so a script around it reads the two apart the same way.
.PP
It exits with \fB130\fR when you gave up with Ctrl+C.
That is 128 plus the signal, the way a shell reports an interrupted command.
.PP
At the end you are told what happened, the way ssh says it:
.PP
.RS
Connection to \fIhost\fR closed.
.RE
.RS
Connection to \fIhost\fR closed by remote host: \fIreason\fR
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B otpme-shell client1
A root shell on client1.
.TP
.B otpme-shell \-\-user alice client1
A shell as alice.
Uses the keys of alice on that host.
.TP
.B otpme-shell \-s munich client2
A root shell on \fBclient2\fR, which is a host of site \fBmunich\fR.
.TP
.B otpme-shell \-\-command 'systemctl status otpme' client1
Run one command and print what it says.
.TP
.B otpme-shell \-\-throwaway\-key \-\-expiry 30s client1
A shell on a key made for this session, and a request that is good for 30 seconds.
Needs a signer with \fB\-\-allow\-throwaway\-key\fR on that host.
.TP
.B otpme-tool show_signer \-\-signer\-type remote_access
On the host: which signers may ask for a shell, and which of them may bring their own key.
.TP
.B otpme-deploy results \fIjob_uuid\fR
What came of a session.
Says who got in, how the session ended and which keys were skipped.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/cache/otpme/ssh/authorized_keys/\fIuser\fI/authorized_keys
The keys the host allows, on the host.
Written by \fBhostd\fR from the SSH tokens of the realm.
.TP
.I /var/log/otpme/confd/\fIjob_uuid\fI.log
The log of one session, on the host.
Never what was said in the session.
.SH NOTES
A session is written into the login history of the host, so
.BR last (1)
there shows it like any other login:
.PP
.RS
.nf
root  pts/3  otpme/edfc63d5  Thu Oct  8 15:53  still logged in
.fi
.RE
.PP
The origin names the job, and the job names the rest: who asked for the shell, which key got in and when it was signed (\fBotpme\-deploy show \fIuuid\fR).
.PP
While it runs \fBlast\fR says \fBstill logged in\fR, because the record names the process of the shell and that one is still there.
A session whose end was never written \(em one that was cut off hard \(em shows as \fBgone \- no logout\fR.
.PP
The shell is a login session of that user for the kernel as well, the same as a login over
.BR ssh (1).
So the audit of the host puts what happens in the session under the user the shell runs as, and not under the daemon that started it.
.PP
It is not in \fBwho\fR(1).
That reads another file, one in which a record has to be found and replaced rather than appended, and which every other login on the host writes too.
.PP
The size of your terminal is sent when the shell starts.
It is not sent again, so a window you resize during a session stays at the size it had.
.PP
Your language is offered to the shell (\fBLANG\fR and the \fBLC_*\fR variables).
Nothing else from your environment is sent.
A shell that ran with the environment of whoever asked for it would run their code.
